<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Right-endpoint approximation</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Estimate the area under the curve of \(f(x)={@f(x)@}\) bound between \([0,{@cc@}]\) using the right-endpoint approximation by dividing the interval \([0,{@cc@}]\) into</p>
<p>a) \(n={@cc@}\) partitions,</p>
<p>\(\Delta x=\frac{b-a}{n}=\)[[input:ans1a]] [[validation:ans1a]] [[feedback:prt1a]]</p>
<p>Now using the right-endpoints of the partitions, the area is approximately,</p>
<p>\(A\approx \displaystyle \sum_{j=1}^{@cc@} f(x_{j}) \Delta x= \) [[input:ans1b]] [[validation:ans1b]] [[feedback:prt1b]]</p>
<p>b) \(n={@2*cc@}\) partitions.</p>
<p>\(\Delta x=\frac{b-a}{n}=\)[[input:ans2a]] [[validation:ans2a]] [[feedback:prt2a]]</p>
<p>Now using the right-endpoints of the partitions, the area is approximately,</p>
<p>\(A\approx \displaystyle \sum_{j=1}^{@2*cc@} f(x_{j}) \Delta x= \) [[input:ans2b]] [[validation:ans2b]]</p>
<p>(<strong>Give your answer correct to 2 decimal places</strong>)[[feedback:prt2b]]</p>
<p>c) By taking limits of the Riemann sum, evaluate the integral \(\displaystyle \int_0^{@cc@}{@f(x)@} dx\)</p>
<p> </p>
<p>1. Recall the Reimann sum formula: \(\displaystyle \int_a^b f(x) dx= \lim_{{n \to \infty}} \sum_{j=1} ^n f(x_j) \Delta x \),</p>
<p>2. Also, you may find these summation formulas helpful:</p>
<p>\(\displaystyle \sum_{j=1}^n j^2 = \frac{n(n+1)(2n+1)}{6}\) and</p>
<p>\(\displaystyle \sum_{j=1}^n j = \frac{n(n+1)}{2}\)</p>
<p>Step 1: Compute the following and fill in the blanks.</p>
<p>\(\displaystyle \Delta x = \frac{b-a}{n} \implies \Delta x = \) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</p>
<p>\(\displaystyle x_j = a+ j\Delta x \implies x_j = \) [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</p>
<p>\(\displaystyle f(x_j) = \) [[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</p>
<p> </p>
<p>\(\displaystyle \sum_{j=1} ^n f(x_j) \Delta x = \) [[input:ans4]] [[validation:ans4]] [[feedback:prt4]]</p>
<p> </p>
<p>Step 2: Therefore, deduce this definite integral using the Reimann sum formula.</p>
<p>\(\displaystyle \lim_{{n \to \infty}} \sum_j ^n f(x_j) \Delta x = \) [[input:ans5]] [[validation:ans5]] [[feedback:prt5]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked solution</h3>
<p>a) \(n={@cc@}\) partitions,</p>
<p>\(\Delta x=\frac{b-a}{n}={@ta1a@}\)</p>
<p>Now using the right-endpoints of the partitions, the area is approximately,</p>
<p>\(A\approx \displaystyle \sum_{j=1}^{@cc@} f(x_{j}) \Delta x=\sum_{j=1}^{@cc@}{@sm1a@} ={@ta1b@} \) </p>
<p>b) \(n={@2*cc@}\) partitions.</p>
<p>\(\Delta x=\frac{b-a}{n}={@ta2a@}\)</p>
<p>Now using the right-endpoints of the partitions, the area is approximately,</p>
<p>\(A\approx \displaystyle \sum_{j=1}^{@2*cc@} f(x_{j}) \Delta x=\sum_{j=1}^{@cc@}{@sm2a@}*\frac{1}{2} ={@ta2b@} \) </p>
<p> </p>
<p>Given that \(\displaystyle \int_a^b f(x) = \lim_{{n \to \infty}} \sum_{j=1} ^n f(x_j) \Delta x \)</p>
<p>Let us go ahead and evaluate this integral \(\displaystyle \int_0^{@cc@}{@funct@}\);</p>
<p>\(\displaystyle \Delta x = \frac{b-a}{n} \implies \Delta x = {@ta1@} \)</p>
<p>\(\displaystyle x_j = a+ j\Delta x \implies x_j = {@ta2@}\)</p>
<p>\(\displaystyle f(x_j) = {@ta3@}\)</p>
<p>\( \displaystyle f(x_j) \Delta x = {@f_xj@}\)</p>
<p> </p>
<p>Now we go ahead and compute \(\displaystyle \sum_j ^n f(x_j)\)</p>
<p>We need to substitute the following when setting up the Reimann sum:</p>
<p>\(\displaystyle \sum_{j=1} ^n j^2 = \frac{n(n+1)(2n+1)}{6}\) and \(\displaystyle \sum_{j=1} ^n j = \frac{n(n+1)}{n}\), we can go ahead and form the Reimann sum as shown,</p>
<p>\(\displaystyle \sum_{j=1} ^n f(x_j) \Delta x = \sum_{j=1}^n {@f_xj@}= {@ta4@}\)</p>
<p> </p>
<p>Finally, taking the limit as \(n\to\infty\),</p>
<p>\(\displaystyle \lim_{{n \to \infty}} \sum_j ^n f(x_j) \Delta x \implies \lim_{{n \to \infty}} {@ta4@} = {@ta5@}\)</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
